// ASSAULT round 2, non-capability lens, resource-dos (2026-08-07).
//
// Distinct from, and DEEPER than, the already-filed
// `attack_mono_struct_doubling_no_depth_guard.kry`: that file confirmed
// (by reading kryos-mir/src/lower.rs) that `enter_mono_frame` -- which
// enforces `MAX_MONO_DEPTH`/`MAX_MONO_TOTAL` -- is called ONLY from
// `monomorphize`/`monomorphize_impl_fn`, never from `monomorphize_struct`
// or `monomorphize_enum` (re-confirmed live this session: grepping
// `enter_mono_frame` in lower.rs finds exactly 2 call sites, at ~16233 and
// ~16445, neither inside `monomorphize_struct` ~15613 or
// `monomorphize_enum` ~15687). BUT that file's own tested shape -- N
// hand-written `let` lines manually doubling a type (`Node<Node<...i64>>>`
// via 10 explicit `Node { a: prev, b: prev }` statements) -- was
// EXPLICITLY RULED OUT for resource-dos ("kryos check and kryos run both
// complete in a few seconds even at depth 10-12, no timeout, no unbounded
// memory") and instead surfaced an unrelated double-free bug.
//
// THIS file tests the shape that gap analysis actually predicts and that
// repro did not try: a GENUINELY SELF-REFERENTIAL generic struct where the
// recursive field wraps the type parameter in ANOTHER generic at each
// level (`Node<T> { next: Option<Node<Wrap<T>>> }`), so the mangled name
// is NEVER the same twice and the existing placeholder-only guard (which
// protects only the EXACT-same-mangled-name case, e.g.
// `Node<T> { next: Option<Node<T>> }`, per lower.rs's own comment at
// monomorphize_struct ~15651) never fires. Unboundedness here comes from
// the TYPE SUBSTITUTION ITSELF, not from source text length: a single
// ~20-line declaration plus ONE trivial instantiation is enough.
//
// VERIFIED LIVE this session, `compiler/target/release/kryos.exe`, no
// compiler changes, `KRYOS_STDLIB_DIR` set per repo convention, via a
// Python subprocess harness (avoids any shell-level timeout ambiguity;
// full command + stdout/stderr captured, tasklist checked after for
// orphans):
//
//   control (examples/hello.kry), `kryos run`:            rc=0, 0.64s
//   THIS FILE, `kryos check` (type-check only):            rc=0, 0.02s,
//                                                            no output
//   THIS FILE, `kryos run` (reaches MIR lowering):          TIMEOUT,
//     first probe:  30s wall,  zero stdout/stderr the whole time
//     second probe: 152.27s wall, zero stdout/stderr the whole time
//   `tasklist` immediately after each kill: no lingering kryos.exe
//     (the Python-side timeout kill is clean, not an orphan-leak issue)
//
// `kryos check` completing in 20ms with zero diagnostics pinpoints the
// hang precisely to MIR lowering / monomorphization (type-checking itself
// is fine -- the program is well-typed), consistent with the missing
// `enter_mono_frame` guard on `monomorphize_struct`: each level's
// `next: Option<Node<Wrap<...>>>` field substitution recurses back into
// `monomorphize_struct` for a distinct, never-cached mangled name, with
// no MAX_MONO_DEPTH-style call-stack cap and (empirically) no timely
// MAX_MONO_TYPE_NODES=4096 trip either -- 152s+ produced neither a crash
// nor the `mono_fatal` "monomorphization limit exceeded" diagnostic that
// the guarded functions (`monomorphize`/`monomorphize_impl_fn`) would
// produce almost instantly at their much lower MAX_MONO_DEPTH=300.
//
// Severity: RESOURCE-DOS. This is not a crafted "bomb" the way item 14's
// stray-`;` flood or the elif-let-chain repro's 50,000-line generated
// file are -- it's a small, idiomatic-looking generic wrapper pattern
// (a generic node type whose recursive field is wrapped in another
// generic, e.g. modelling a growing capability/decoration chain) that
// ANY developer could write by accident, with zero adversarial intent,
// and it hangs the compiler on `kryos run`/`kryos build` (both reach MIR
// lowering) for as long as it's left running -- not diagnosed further
// this session (no compiler changes, attacker-only mandate). Fix shape:
// the same `enter_mono_frame`/`exit_mono_frame` pair already used by
// `monomorphize`/`monomorphize_impl_fn`, added around the recursive field
// -- and variant -- substitution calls inside `monomorphize_struct` and
// `monomorphize_enum`.

use std::option::{Option, Some, None}

struct Wrap<T> {
    val: T,
}

struct Node<T> {
    depth: i64,
    next: Option<Node<Wrap<T>>>,
}

fn main() {
    let n: Node<i64> = Node { depth: 0, next: None() }
    println("built depth 0: " + to_string(n.depth))
}
